```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on mswitch0, the Campus 1 multilayer switch — not on router11 as the
engineer believed. mswitch0's OSPF daemon (ospfd) has been killed: the frr.service
is inactive/dead, no ospfd process appears in ps, and /etc/frr/ospfd.conf is an
empty file. The startup.sh confirms that ospfd was intended to run and to advertise
four networks into OSPF area 0 (10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24,
172.16.30.0/24), but those advertisements are no longer being made.

Because ospfd is down, mswitch0 holds only its four directly connected kernel
routes. When any Campus 1 PC (PC5–PC14 on 172.16.10–30.x, using mswitch0's VLAN
SVIs as their default gateway) sends traffic toward Campus 2 (10.5.0.x, 10.6.0.x)
or the backbone, mswitch0 immediately returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable"
(confirmed by live pings from pc5 to 10.5.0.1 and 172.30.1.2). Intra-Campus-1
traffic still works because all three VLAN subnets are directly connected on
mswitch0. Router16, the upstream L3 router, has lost its OSPF peer on bond0 —
router16's OSPF neighbor table shows only router7 — so the forward return path
from Campus 2 back to 172.16.x.x is also broken.

Router11 is NOT faulty. Its routing table contains all 172.16.10/20/30.0/24
prefixes; the engineer's premise is incorrect and restarting router11 would have
no effect.

Root cause: the ospfd process on mswitch0 was killed, eliminating mswitch0's
participation in OSPF and leaving it without any route to external networks.

Fix: on mswitch0, restart FRR (`/etc/init.d/frr start`), then re-apply the OSPF
network statements via vtysh (network 10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10–30.0/24 all in
area 0), and run `write memory` to repopulate ospfd.conf. Once ospfd forms an
adjacency with router16, mswitch0 will learn external routes and re-advertise
Campus 1 prefixes, restoring full Campus 1 ↔ Campus 2 reachability.
=== END ===
```